1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,000
The task monumental and for many, deadly.

2
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:09,000
Building the world's largest dam in one of the most unforgiving

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:12,000
climates on Earth. Nothing like it had ever been

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:18,000
attempted. Now, Hoover Dam on Modern Marvents.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,300
Located in the middle of America's southwestern desert,

6
00:00:33,400 --> 00:00:36,400
stands a true giant in the history of human ingenuity

7
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:38,940
and accomplishment.

8
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:42,680
Built in the 1930s, Hoover Dam holds back trillions

9
00:00:42,780 --> 00:00:45,040
of gallons of life-sustaining water,

10
00:00:45,140 --> 00:00:48,780
provides prodigious amounts of hydroelectric power,

11
00:00:48,880 --> 00:00:54,160
and inspires awe as a structure of monumental proportions.

12
00:00:54,260 --> 00:01:00,000
At 726 feet, Hoover Dam is as tall as a skyscraper.

13
00:01:00,001 --> 00:01:03,600
At the base of the dam, the concrete is an incredible

14
00:01:03,700 --> 00:01:07,600
660 feet thick.

15
00:01:07,700 --> 00:01:10,440
The amount of concrete used to construct Hoover Dam

16
00:01:10,540 --> 00:01:15,940
could build a two-lane highway from Los Angeles to Boston.

17
00:01:16,040 --> 00:01:19,060
While from the outside, the dam appears to be a solid wall

18
00:01:19,160 --> 00:01:23,060
of concrete, inside it's laced with two miles of corridors

19
00:01:23,160 --> 00:01:23,960
and tunnels.

20
00:01:23,961 --> 00:01:24,661
tunnels.

21
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:30,600
The dam holds back nearly two years

22
00:01:30,601 --> 00:01:33,540
of the Colorado River's average flow

23
00:01:33,541 --> 00:01:37,300
and creates 110 mile long Lake Mead.

24
00:01:37,301 --> 00:01:40,280
It's America's largest man-made reservoir

25
00:01:40,281 --> 00:01:43,880
and is located in one of the driest places on Earth,

26
00:01:43,881 --> 00:01:46,380
30 miles southeast of Las Vegas, Nevada.

27
00:01:53,960 --> 00:02:02,720
The primary reasons the dam was built were flood control and to guarantee a steady water supply to the quickly growing population in the southwestern United States.

28
00:02:05,700 --> 00:02:12,760
Generating electricity was secondary, but now much of the modern southwest depends on the dam to feed its power-hungry lifestyle.

29
00:02:16,680 --> 00:02:22,720
To generate electricity, water is fed into 30-foot diameter pipes called penstocks.

30
00:02:23,960 --> 00:02:27,360
the four intake towers standing in Lake Mead.

31
00:02:27,460 --> 00:02:29,460
Once it goes into the 30-foot diameter water pipe,

32
00:02:29,560 --> 00:02:31,760
the water's traveling at about 12 miles an hour,

33
00:02:31,860 --> 00:02:33,700
which is fast for water, but not fast enough

34
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:35,200
to turn our generators.

35
00:02:35,300 --> 00:02:38,500
The rotating weight of the generators itself is 800 tons.

36
00:02:38,600 --> 00:02:40,000
So we need to increase the speed,

37
00:02:40,100 --> 00:02:42,720
and we do that by continuously reducing the size of the pipe.

38
00:02:42,820 --> 00:02:44,520
30 foot becomes 13.

39
00:02:44,620 --> 00:02:46,520
13 narrows down to a scroll case, which

40
00:02:46,620 --> 00:02:48,020
is four feet in diameter.

41
00:02:48,120 --> 00:02:50,120
By the time the water actually reaches the turbine,

42
00:02:50,220 --> 00:02:53,960
it's going approximately 85 miles an hour.

43
00:02:53,961 --> 00:02:58,040
Eventually, it hits the turbine runner, which I've got one out for maintenance here in the background.

44
00:02:58,700 --> 00:03:00,980
This turbine runner weighs 35 tons.

45
00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:02,120
It's stainless steel.

46
00:03:02,620 --> 00:03:08,980
The water impacting that turbine runner causes it to rotate 180 RPMs 180 times per minute.

47
00:03:09,720 --> 00:03:13,660
That rotation is transferred through a solid shaft up to our generator rotor.

48
00:03:14,840 --> 00:03:20,780
When you push a generator, what you're doing is you're moving magnetic fields across wires.

49
00:03:20,781 --> 00:03:24,180
We have our big electromagnets on the rotor of the machine,

50
00:03:24,280 --> 00:03:27,620
and then we have stationary wires that we call the stator.

51
00:03:27,720 --> 00:03:31,220
And when those magnetic fields cross across the stator,

52
00:03:31,320 --> 00:03:34,820
it pushes electrons through those big wires in the stator,

53
00:03:34,920 --> 00:03:38,000
and that's the electrons on their way to our transformer

54
00:03:38,100 --> 00:03:40,440
and out to the world.

55
00:03:40,540 --> 00:03:42,540
The electricity leaves the transformers

56
00:03:42,640 --> 00:03:46,340
on power lines strung up along the sides of the canyon.

57
00:03:46,440 --> 00:03:48,900
Behind me, you'll see what they call cantilever towers,

58
00:03:48,901 --> 00:03:50,940
and they appear to be falling into the canyon,

59
00:03:51,040 --> 00:03:52,100
but they're not.

60
00:03:52,200 --> 00:03:53,240
They're actually leaning into the canyon,

61
00:03:53,340 --> 00:03:54,840
and they're designed to be that way.

62
00:03:54,940 --> 00:03:56,740
What we want to do is take the power lines up

63
00:03:56,840 --> 00:03:59,080
and over the canyon walls without having them touch

64
00:03:59,180 --> 00:04:00,440
the same canyon walls.

65
00:04:00,540 --> 00:04:01,840
Otherwise, they would shore it out.

66
00:04:01,940 --> 00:04:03,740
And that's mainly due to the ferrous material that's

67
00:04:03,840 --> 00:04:06,560
inside those canyon walls.

68
00:04:06,660 --> 00:04:08,780
In other words, there's enough iron content

69
00:04:08,880 --> 00:04:11,720
in the canyon walls to cause the electricity to arc off

70
00:04:11,820 --> 00:04:14,120
the cables if they were allowed to get too close.

71
00:04:14,121 --> 00:04:20,720
There were 17 generators in Hoover Dam's huge power plant,

72
00:04:20,721 --> 00:04:25,860
and they produce more than 4 billion kilowatt hours a year.

73
00:04:25,861 --> 00:04:29,060
We have a service area, including all of Southern

74
00:04:29,061 --> 00:04:33,780
California and Arizona, probably more than 20 million people that

75
00:04:33,781 --> 00:04:36,280
receive some of the power.

76
00:04:36,281 --> 00:04:40,480
Despite Hoover Dam's massive size, like so many of the

77
00:04:40,481 --> 00:04:44,120
construction projects of the 1930s, there's an element

78
00:04:44,121 --> 00:04:50,360
built into it. Notable artists brought a touch

79
00:04:50,361 --> 00:04:53,360
of class to this once bleak and desolate canyon.

80
00:04:57,360 --> 00:05:02,560
Inside, Art Deco design flourishes are found in the ornate visitor

81
00:05:02,561 --> 00:05:07,120
elevators, gleaming aluminum doors, and decorative railings

82
00:05:07,121 --> 00:05:11,880
in the powerhouse. Inlaid in the terrazzo floors

83
00:05:11,881 --> 00:05:15,680
inspired by Native American art that symbolize the power of the

84
00:05:15,681 --> 00:05:17,920
dam.

85
00:05:17,921 --> 00:05:20,680
At the halfway point along one of the main corridors that runs

86
00:05:20,681 --> 00:05:24,720
through the dam, a design marks the border between Arizona and

87
00:05:24,721 --> 00:05:25,720
Nevada.

88
00:05:29,240 --> 00:05:32,540
Hoover Dam epitomizes humanity's discontent with accepting

89
00:05:32,541 --> 00:05:35,240
nature's rules.

90
00:05:35,241 --> 00:05:39,640
It transformed the environment around it.

91
00:05:39,641 --> 00:05:43,640
Building the dam was a colossal undertaking in 1931.

92
00:05:43,740 --> 00:05:47,880
It was the largest government project ever attempted.

93
00:05:47,980 --> 00:05:50,760
Constructed in the depths of the Depression,

94
00:05:50,860 --> 00:05:54,280
it promised to change Barrett to Bountable.

95
00:05:54,380 --> 00:05:56,820
The Southwest is an arid area.

96
00:05:56,920 --> 00:05:59,300
And without a dam like Hoover Dam

97
00:05:59,400 --> 00:06:02,300
to control the flow of the river and make the water available

98
00:06:02,400 --> 00:06:04,300
when it's needed, we just couldn't

99
00:06:04,400 --> 00:06:06,660
have the level of economic development

100
00:06:06,760 --> 00:06:09,640
that we have in the Southwest today.

101
00:06:09,641 --> 00:06:16,640
Hoover Dam was the first major attempt to tame the mighty Colorado River, the same river that carved the Grand Canyon.

102
00:06:19,640 --> 00:06:27,640
Snow melting in the Rocky Mountains sends a torrent of water down the Colorado every spring, but then dries to a trickle by summer.

103
00:06:27,641 --> 00:06:31,780
trickle by summer. The 1,400-mile-long river

104
00:06:31,781 --> 00:06:39,780
drains water from seven states, Wyoming, Nevada, Utah, Colorado,

105
00:06:39,781 --> 00:06:45,400
New Mexico, Arizona, and California, and empties into the

106
00:06:45,401 --> 00:06:49,600
Gulf of California. The early settlers who were

107
00:06:49,601 --> 00:06:53,500
trying to build an economy, build lives at the bottom end of this

108
00:06:53,501 --> 00:06:56,900
river, they planted their crops and built their fields and built

109
00:06:56,901 --> 00:06:59,460
homes and businesses, in the spring times,

110
00:06:59,461 --> 00:07:03,240
those would be subject to inundation or destruction

111
00:07:03,241 --> 00:07:05,700
because of the floods coming down the river.

112
00:07:05,701 --> 00:07:08,640
And by the end of the summer, when the farmers really

113
00:07:08,641 --> 00:07:11,480
needed that water to get their crops to maturity,

114
00:07:11,481 --> 00:07:14,080
they couldn't get to the water.

115
00:07:14,081 --> 00:07:16,520
Attempts to harness the power of the Colorado River

116
00:07:16,521 --> 00:07:20,260
date back more than a century.

117
00:07:20,261 --> 00:07:25,020
In 1901, a private company constructed a canal system

118
00:07:25,021 --> 00:07:28,720
water from the Colorado River and delivered it to the arid

119
00:07:28,721 --> 00:07:32,020
but fertile Imperial Valley of Southern California.

120
00:07:32,021 --> 00:07:34,860
The company cut an opening in the western bank of the Colorado

121
00:07:34,861 --> 00:07:40,260
River and installed a head gate, which controlled water flow.

122
00:07:40,261 --> 00:07:42,980
Initially, the system of small gates and channels worked

123
00:07:42,981 --> 00:07:46,980
beyond expectation. Fields produced a bounty

124
00:07:46,981 --> 00:07:51,980
of agricultural products. Soon, thousands of people flocked

125
00:07:51,981 --> 00:07:54,780
flocked to the Imperial Valley with dreams of finding a new

126
00:07:54,880 --> 00:07:57,280
Eden.

127
00:07:57,380 --> 00:08:02,120
But their dreams soon turned into a nightmare.

128
00:08:02,220 --> 00:08:04,920
The problem was these were not very well engineered works,

129
00:08:05,020 --> 00:08:06,120
and they weren't very good.

130
00:08:06,220 --> 00:08:10,140
And the Colorado River in 1905 had a major flood come down

131
00:08:10,240 --> 00:08:13,440
the river, and it washed out these head works and flowed

132
00:08:13,540 --> 00:08:16,340
into the Imperial Valley for 18 months.

133
00:08:16,440 --> 00:08:19,240
They essentially created what is today the Salton Sea,

134
00:08:19,241 --> 00:08:23,440
much of the agricultural land, destroyed homes, destroyed

135
00:08:23,540 --> 00:08:26,740
businesses, really wreaked havoc.

136
00:08:26,840 --> 00:08:29,580
If the breach wasn't repaired, experts

137
00:08:29,680 --> 00:08:33,580
predicted the Salton Sea would continue to grow and eventually

138
00:08:33,680 --> 00:08:38,500
drown millions of acres of California and Arizona.

139
00:08:38,600 --> 00:08:42,000
It wasn't until early 1907 that the breach was successfully

140
00:08:42,100 --> 00:08:45,800
closed, but it was considered only a temporary solution.

141
00:08:45,801 --> 00:08:50,600
In 1922, Arthur Powell Davis, director of the US Bureau

142
00:08:50,700 --> 00:08:54,040
of Reclamation, recommended a series of reservoirs

143
00:08:54,140 --> 00:08:55,740
to control the entire river.

144
00:08:58,240 --> 00:09:01,840
Known as the Fall Davis Report, the centerpiece of the plan

145
00:09:01,940 --> 00:09:04,920
was a colossal dam to be built in Boulder Canyon

146
00:09:05,020 --> 00:09:06,360
between Nevada and Arizona.

147
00:09:10,300 --> 00:09:12,360
Before the measure could proceed,

148
00:09:12,460 --> 00:09:15,800
the states had to decide on an equitable division of water.

149
00:09:15,801 --> 00:09:20,600
Arizona believed California was trying to steal an unfair share

150
00:09:20,601 --> 00:09:25,040
of water and blocked legislation for several years.

151
00:09:25,041 --> 00:09:28,040
There's always a fight over water in the West.

152
00:09:28,041 --> 00:09:32,040
When water is scarce, there's going to be tension over water,

153
00:09:32,041 --> 00:09:34,960
and that was the case years ago.

154
00:09:34,961 --> 00:09:37,460
It's still the case today.

155
00:09:37,461 --> 00:09:42,100
Finally, in 1929, the Boulder Canyon Project Act

156
00:09:42,101 --> 00:09:45,800
passed in Congress.

157
00:09:45,801 --> 00:09:55,560
The Bureau of Reclamation worked to design a structure capable of holding back trillions of gallons of water in a very high yet narrow canyon.

158
00:09:57,340 --> 00:10:00,920
They considered many different designs before finding the answer.

159
00:10:03,560 --> 00:10:08,180
Hoover Dam is a combination of gravity and arch.

160
00:10:08,181 --> 00:10:19,860
Gravity dams are dams that are just so massive that they can, their weight of them themselves can hold back the weight of the water behind them.

161
00:10:20,060 --> 00:10:26,760
And then arch dams traditionally are built in the shape of an arch and they're actually keyed into canyon walls.

162
00:10:27,020 --> 00:10:32,700
The weight of the water presses the arch into the sides of the canyon walls and there's strength in the arch.

163
00:10:32,701 --> 00:10:34,240
It's kind of like an egg shell.

164
00:10:34,340 --> 00:10:36,440
If you try to press in on the edge of an egg,

165
00:10:36,540 --> 00:10:37,940
an egg shell is very thin.

166
00:10:38,040 --> 00:10:39,740
But the egg itself is very strong.

167
00:10:39,840 --> 00:10:43,080
If you try to press in on the edge of a, on the two ends

168
00:10:43,180 --> 00:10:45,440
of an egg, you'll find that you can't crush an egg.

169
00:10:45,540 --> 00:10:48,380
It's the strength of the arch.

170
00:10:48,480 --> 00:10:52,180
The Bureau of Reclamation's planned Arch Gravity Dam

171
00:10:52,280 --> 00:10:55,160
would not only be the largest dam in the world,

172
00:10:55,260 --> 00:10:59,420
it was to be built in one of the most desolate areas on Earth.

173
00:10:59,520 --> 00:11:02,460
I sometimes question the audacity of the people

174
00:11:02,461 --> 00:11:05,160
the people who had the courage and the conviction

175
00:11:05,161 --> 00:11:07,660
to think they could build a dam out in the middle of a desert

176
00:11:07,661 --> 00:11:08,660
where nothing existed.

177
00:11:08,661 --> 00:11:10,800
There were no roads to the site.

178
00:11:10,801 --> 00:11:13,140
There was no power to the site.

179
00:11:13,141 --> 00:11:15,380
There was no labor force here.

180
00:11:15,381 --> 00:11:17,700
There was nowhere for the people to live who were going

181
00:11:17,701 --> 00:11:19,600
to come and build the dam.

182
00:11:19,601 --> 00:11:21,720
In spite of all the obstacles,

183
00:11:21,721 --> 00:11:23,740
the dam had to be perfect.

184
00:11:23,741 --> 00:11:26,960
Surveyors decided to move the location from Boulder Canyon

185
00:11:26,961 --> 00:11:30,020
to Black Canyon, eight miles downstream.

186
00:11:30,021 --> 00:11:32,220
Black Canyon, the site where we actually are today,

187
00:11:32,221 --> 00:11:35,220
mostly because of the fissures that are underneath where we

188
00:11:35,221 --> 00:11:36,220
are today.

189
00:11:36,221 --> 00:11:38,220
There were less fissures here in this area.

190
00:11:38,221 --> 00:11:41,220
Now, the rock is some of the best rock that you can find for

191
00:11:41,221 --> 00:11:42,220
placing a dam.

192
00:11:42,221 --> 00:11:43,220
It's called Andesite Brescia.

193
00:11:43,221 --> 00:11:46,220
It actually has a tighter granular composition than

194
00:11:46,221 --> 00:11:48,220
granite.

195
00:11:48,221 --> 00:11:52,220
The location was set, but the task ahead was great.

196
00:11:52,221 --> 00:11:56,220
Hire an army of thousands, divert the river around the

197
00:11:56,221 --> 00:12:01,220
dam site, and form a mountain of concrete into a dam, all

198
00:12:01,221 --> 00:12:04,360
all in the middle of nowhere.

199
00:12:04,460 --> 00:12:05,660
One question remained.

200
00:12:08,460 --> 00:12:09,460
Could it be done?

201
00:12:12,960 --> 00:12:15,460
Lake Mead, the reservoir created by Hoover Dam,

202
00:12:15,560 --> 00:12:19,380
contains enough water to cover the entire state of Pennsylvania

203
00:12:19,480 --> 00:12:21,080
with one foot of water.

204
00:12:31,220 --> 00:12:33,220
Hoover Dam.

205
00:12:42,360 --> 00:12:45,960
Hoover Dam may be an icon of American ingenuity,

206
00:12:45,961 --> 00:12:49,340
but the structure itself is only the end product

207
00:12:49,341 --> 00:12:53,340
of an elaborate engineering, logistical, and human story.

208
00:12:55,980 --> 00:12:59,420
In 1930, the Bureau of Reclamation,

209
00:12:59,421 --> 00:13:03,420
responsible for water management in the Western United States,

210
00:13:03,520 --> 00:13:06,520
had to first build a railway supply line from Las Vegas

211
00:13:06,620 --> 00:13:10,260
to Black Canyon, the site chosen for the new dam.

212
00:13:12,760 --> 00:13:15,460
Although the site had changed from Boulder Canyon to Black

213
00:13:15,560 --> 00:13:19,180
Canyon, the endeavor was still called the Boulder Canyon

214
00:13:19,280 --> 00:13:20,280
Project.

215
00:13:20,380 --> 00:13:23,980
People naturally anticipated the name would be Boulder Dam.

216
00:13:24,080 --> 00:13:27,780
But at a railroad spike driving ceremony, Ray Wilbur,

217
00:13:27,781 --> 00:13:30,440
Ray Wilbur, Secretary of the Interior under President

218
00:13:30,540 --> 00:13:34,620
Herbert Hoover, set the record straight.

219
00:13:34,720 --> 00:13:37,820
The Secretary of the Interior, Ray Lyman Wilbur,

220
00:13:37,920 --> 00:13:39,720
came out to drive the Silver Spike

221
00:13:39,820 --> 00:13:42,920
to begin construction of the railroad spur from Las Vegas

222
00:13:43,020 --> 00:13:45,540
out to Boulder City town site.

223
00:13:45,640 --> 00:13:48,240
And that day is the day that he announced, well, we're going

224
00:13:48,340 --> 00:13:50,040
to call it Hoover Dam.

225
00:13:50,140 --> 00:13:53,540
Well, this was a year after the stock market had crashed

226
00:13:53,640 --> 00:13:55,140
and the Depression was settling in

227
00:13:55,141 --> 00:13:57,840
and people were not at all happy with Hoover.

228
00:13:57,841 --> 00:14:01,180
Despite the public's disapproval of President Hoover,

229
00:14:01,181 --> 00:14:04,040
they welcomed the new job opportunities.

230
00:14:04,041 --> 00:14:07,080
People were desperate for work.

231
00:14:07,081 --> 00:14:09,780
Soon, thousands poured into the Las Vegas area

232
00:14:09,781 --> 00:14:12,000
in hopes of finding employment.

233
00:14:12,001 --> 00:14:13,900
Before the construction started,

234
00:14:13,901 --> 00:14:17,400
before they hired anybody, they came because they knew

235
00:14:17,401 --> 00:14:23,600
there was a chance of a job, just a rumor of a job.

236
00:14:23,601 --> 00:14:26,260
Before any of today's social programs,

237
00:14:26,360 --> 00:14:29,840
the only choice the unemployed had was to search out work,

238
00:14:29,940 --> 00:14:33,240
wherever it might be.

239
00:14:33,340 --> 00:14:35,840
Unfortunately, the dam was to be built in one of the most

240
00:14:35,940 --> 00:14:38,540
inhospitable climates in the world,

241
00:14:38,640 --> 00:14:42,460
where temperatures regularly reached 120 degrees,

242
00:14:42,560 --> 00:14:46,560
and shelter simply didn't exist.

243
00:14:46,660 --> 00:14:49,260
Desperate people brought their entire families.

244
00:14:49,360 --> 00:14:51,900
They set up makeshift camps or lived in their cars

245
00:14:51,901 --> 00:14:52,900
while waiting for work.

246
00:14:56,200 --> 00:14:58,600
Unsanitary living conditions and heat

247
00:14:58,700 --> 00:15:01,640
claimed several lives that first summer.

248
00:15:01,740 --> 00:15:04,040
The government planned to build Boulder City,

249
00:15:04,140 --> 00:15:08,140
an elaborate construction camp for its workers.

250
00:15:08,240 --> 00:15:11,460
But it wouldn't be built for more than a year.

251
00:15:11,560 --> 00:15:17,200
Problem was that the depression caught the government off guard.

252
00:15:17,300 --> 00:15:20,860
They had hoped that they would be able to have the city built

253
00:15:20,861 --> 00:15:23,760
and finished before people could move in,

254
00:15:23,860 --> 00:15:26,000
and then they would start work on the dam.

255
00:15:26,100 --> 00:15:30,900
But the stock market crashed, and people started moving in.

256
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:35,200
On January 10, 1931, the true scope of the project

257
00:15:35,300 --> 00:15:37,980
became public knowledge.

258
00:15:38,080 --> 00:15:40,220
The Bureau of Reclamation supplied plans

259
00:15:40,320 --> 00:15:42,380
to any company interested in placing a bid

260
00:15:42,480 --> 00:15:45,320
on the construction of the dam.

261
00:15:45,420 --> 00:15:48,220
And it seemed everyone was interested in what was sure

262
00:15:48,221 --> 00:15:52,820
to be the largest government contract ever awarded.

263
00:15:52,920 --> 00:15:56,720
Blueprints detailed an immense structure.

264
00:15:56,820 --> 00:15:58,460
The process of dam building called

265
00:15:58,560 --> 00:16:00,860
for three enormous undertakings.

266
00:16:00,960 --> 00:16:04,580
First, tremendous tunnels over 50 feet in diameter

267
00:16:04,680 --> 00:16:07,180
were needed to divert the course of the river.

268
00:16:07,280 --> 00:16:10,980
Then temporary cofferdams would be erected to provide a dry work

269
00:16:11,080 --> 00:16:12,380
area.

270
00:16:12,480 --> 00:16:16,320
And finally, 3 and 1 quarter million cubic yards of concrete

271
00:16:16,321 --> 00:16:19,280
would be poured to build the dam itself.

272
00:16:19,281 --> 00:16:21,860
The sheer size of the project intimidated even the most

273
00:16:21,861 --> 00:16:24,080
confident contractors.

274
00:16:24,081 --> 00:16:26,080
The challenge was monumental.

275
00:16:26,081 --> 00:16:28,660
Build the world's largest dam in the middle of the desert

276
00:16:28,661 --> 00:16:30,620
in less than seven years.

277
00:16:30,621 --> 00:16:32,920
The government imposed deadline.

278
00:16:32,921 --> 00:16:35,660
Back in the 30s, there was no one company that

279
00:16:35,661 --> 00:16:38,420
could accomplish the building of the Hoover Dam.

280
00:16:38,421 --> 00:16:40,760
It was such a massive project.

281
00:16:40,761 --> 00:16:43,480
So six individual companies actually got together

282
00:16:43,481 --> 00:16:45,060
and formed one entity.

283
00:16:45,061 --> 00:16:48,600
And they called themselves Six Companies.

284
00:16:48,700 --> 00:16:50,900
All of the firms that made up Six Companies

285
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:53,200
were familiar with big projects.

286
00:16:53,300 --> 00:16:56,800
They had independently shaped the West, building railroads,

287
00:16:56,900 --> 00:17:02,120
tunnels, bridges, and highways.

288
00:17:02,220 --> 00:17:05,220
Between the six, perhaps they had enough experience

289
00:17:05,320 --> 00:17:09,420
and resources to build a dam of such great magnitude.

290
00:17:09,520 --> 00:17:12,120
But they needed someone who was respected in the field of dam

291
00:17:12,121 --> 00:17:14,860
construction to oversee the project.

292
00:17:14,960 --> 00:17:19,120
And they chose Frank Crow to be their foreman here,

293
00:17:19,220 --> 00:17:21,160
the superintendent of the job.

294
00:17:21,260 --> 00:17:23,660
And he was a hands-on foreman.

295
00:17:23,760 --> 00:17:28,460
He didn't spend much time behind his desk.

296
00:17:28,560 --> 00:17:32,180
Frank Crow was the most respected dam builder in America.

297
00:17:32,280 --> 00:17:36,680
Born in 1882, he was a seasoned veteran with more than 20 years

298
00:17:36,780 --> 00:17:40,920
of experience and had developed important time-saving and innovative

299
00:17:40,921 --> 00:17:45,920
methods. With Crow on board, six companies

300
00:17:45,921 --> 00:17:48,920
was ready to make a bid for the dam construction.

301
00:17:48,921 --> 00:17:54,920
On March 4, 1931, the Bureau of Reclamation opened sealed bids.

302
00:17:54,921 --> 00:17:58,920
Six companies' firm won the contract with the lowest bid

303
00:17:58,921 --> 00:18:05,920
of $48,890,955.

304
00:18:05,921 --> 00:18:06,820
Let it go!

305
00:18:06,821 --> 00:18:07,820
Let it go!

306
00:18:07,821 --> 00:18:13,420
Blasting began almost immediately.

307
00:18:13,421 --> 00:18:16,560
Six companies had their work cut out for them.

308
00:18:16,561 --> 00:18:19,800
Initially, even getting to the dam site was an obstacle.

309
00:18:19,801 --> 00:18:21,240
As there were no roads from the rim

310
00:18:21,241 --> 00:18:24,680
to the bottom of the canyon, men had to ride ferries

311
00:18:24,681 --> 00:18:26,840
to the dam site.

312
00:18:26,841 --> 00:18:29,940
Later, cableways were erected across the canyon

313
00:18:29,941 --> 00:18:33,920
to get men and materials to the bottom.

314
00:18:33,921 --> 00:18:37,460
The first monumental task was the diversion of the river

315
00:18:37,560 --> 00:18:40,920
around the dam site. Engineers needed a dry work area

316
00:18:41,020 --> 00:18:45,460
in which to set the dam. Four enormous tunnels

317
00:18:45,560 --> 00:18:47,160
had to be drilled through the canyon walls

318
00:18:47,260 --> 00:18:51,580
to circumvent the site. The tunnels would have to carry

319
00:18:51,680 --> 00:18:56,080
the entire volume of the Colorado River.

320
00:18:56,180 --> 00:18:59,020
To add to the pressure, the Bureau of Reclamation

321
00:18:59,120 --> 00:19:01,720
initiated stiff fines for missed deadlines

322
00:19:01,721 --> 00:19:02,960
at each stage of construction.

323
00:19:06,860 --> 00:19:10,860
Workmen drilled blasting holes and loaded them with dynamite.

324
00:19:10,960 --> 00:19:13,800
A special three-story truck holding drillers

325
00:19:13,900 --> 00:19:16,160
at three different levels expedited the process.

326
00:19:21,280 --> 00:19:24,000
An endless stream of trucks removed blasted rock

327
00:19:24,100 --> 00:19:27,440
from the diversion tunnels and labored up the steep incline

328
00:19:27,540 --> 00:19:29,540
for disposal.

329
00:19:29,541 --> 00:19:33,640
Three shifts of men worked 24 hours a day, seven days a week,

330
00:19:33,641 --> 00:19:36,040
to keep the tunnels on schedule.

331
00:19:36,041 --> 00:19:39,380
Frank Crowe was responsible for keeping things moving.

332
00:19:39,381 --> 00:19:41,480
And so it was hurry up, hurry up, hurry up.

333
00:19:41,481 --> 00:19:44,880
And in fact, his nickname was Hurry Up Crowe.

334
00:19:44,881 --> 00:19:47,300
We've got to get all this done.

335
00:19:47,301 --> 00:19:51,800
He was really was a genius in his way.

336
00:19:51,801 --> 00:19:55,900
The hectic pace of construction had its consequences.

337
00:19:55,901 --> 00:19:59,600
Heat exhaustion cost 14 men their lives.

338
00:19:59,700 --> 00:20:01,600
After the depression and the crash,

339
00:20:01,700 --> 00:20:03,800
it was so difficult to get a job.

340
00:20:03,900 --> 00:20:06,840
Companies like the six companies could easily afford

341
00:20:06,940 --> 00:20:10,240
to cut a lot of corners in terms of safety and health

342
00:20:10,340 --> 00:20:12,960
of their workers, in terms of pay, in terms of comfort,

343
00:20:13,060 --> 00:20:14,060
and that kind of thing.

344
00:20:14,160 --> 00:20:15,160
And they did.

345
00:20:15,260 --> 00:20:19,860
Tensions came to a head in the scorching summer of 1931.

346
00:20:19,960 --> 00:20:23,960
Striking workers demanded better working conditions, fresh water,

347
00:20:23,961 --> 00:20:26,800
and improved safety precautions.

348
00:20:26,900 --> 00:20:30,900
Six companies responded by firing 1,400 strikers

349
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:33,100
and hiring fresh workers readily available

350
00:20:33,200 --> 00:20:34,100
because of the depression.

351
00:20:36,940 --> 00:20:40,920
I came here in August in 1931, and I

352
00:20:41,020 --> 00:20:43,720
worked as a truck driver down at the dam site.

353
00:20:43,820 --> 00:20:47,720
I remember one stretch of 10 months without a day off,

354
00:20:47,820 --> 00:20:51,020
and we were working seven days a week.

355
00:20:51,021 --> 00:20:53,920
And the pay was, to me, was great.

356
00:20:54,020 --> 00:20:56,220
It was $5 a day.

357
00:20:56,320 --> 00:20:58,620
More than 5,000 men worked on the dam

358
00:20:58,720 --> 00:21:01,260
at the height of construction.

359
00:21:01,360 --> 00:21:04,360
Frank Crowe's hardworking team completed the diversion tunnels

360
00:21:04,460 --> 00:21:07,400
months ahead of schedule.

361
00:21:07,500 --> 00:21:09,500
After digging the diversion tunnels,

362
00:21:09,600 --> 00:21:12,400
workers lined them with three feet of concrete.

363
00:21:12,500 --> 00:21:14,580
Special forms of gigantic proportion

364
00:21:14,680 --> 00:21:18,240
moved along the interior of the tunnels.

365
00:21:18,340 --> 00:21:21,020
But the job had just begun, and the most spectacular

366
00:21:21,021 --> 00:21:23,020
spectacular work was yet to come.

367
00:21:27,020 --> 00:21:30,860
Roughly 100 workers lost their lives creating Hoover Dam.

368
00:21:30,861 --> 00:21:35,120
Coincidentally, the first and last persons killed on sight,

369
00:21:35,121 --> 00:21:39,580
J.G. Tierney and P.W. Tierney, were father and son.

370
00:21:39,581 --> 00:21:42,420
The first tours were given in 1937.

371
00:21:42,421 --> 00:21:47,340
As of 2005, it's estimated that more than 39 million people

372
00:21:47,341 --> 00:21:51,360
have come all the way to Black Canyon to experience the dam.

373
00:21:51,361 --> 00:21:54,680
They get a sense of the way to the city of Black Canyon

374
00:21:54,681 --> 00:21:56,820
and to the city of Black Canyon.

375
00:21:56,821 --> 00:22:00,340
And they're all the way to the city of Black Canyon.

376
00:22:00,341 --> 00:22:04,140
And they're all the way to the city of Black Canyon

377
00:22:04,141 --> 00:22:06,680
to experience the dam.

378
00:22:06,681 --> 00:22:10,680
They get a sense of awe, the amount of effort that it took

379
00:22:10,780 --> 00:22:13,480
to build such a massive structure.

380
00:22:13,580 --> 00:22:15,620
Back in the 30s, they didn't have computers.

381
00:22:15,720 --> 00:22:18,820
You know, they had a slide ruler, a pencil, and eraser.

382
00:22:18,920 --> 00:22:22,860
There was just a huge amount of blood, sweat, and tears

383
00:22:22,960 --> 00:22:26,640
literally put into the building of Uwe Dam.

384
00:22:26,740 --> 00:22:28,960
Hanging on the parking structure

385
00:22:29,060 --> 00:22:32,600
is a sculpture of a high scaler, one of the dam builders

386
00:22:32,700 --> 00:22:36,680
who prepared the canyon walls for construction.

387
00:22:36,681 --> 00:22:39,440
It pays tribute to the thousands of workers who came to this

388
00:22:39,540 --> 00:22:43,620
rugged and desolate place to work on the dam.

389
00:22:43,720 --> 00:22:46,420
A plaque on the pillar lists those who lost their lives

390
00:22:46,520 --> 00:22:51,420
during the difficult and often dangerous construction.

391
00:22:51,520 --> 00:22:57,340
No job was more precarious than that of the high scalers.

392
00:22:57,440 --> 00:23:00,380
The jagged canyon walls required smoothing and shaping

393
00:23:00,480 --> 00:23:02,240
for the abutting dam.

394
00:23:02,340 --> 00:23:04,380
And thousands of cubic yards of rock

395
00:23:04,381 --> 00:23:07,720
needed to be blasted from the sheer canyon faces.

396
00:23:07,820 --> 00:23:12,120
In daring acrobatic displays, high scalers swung from ropes

397
00:23:12,220 --> 00:23:15,320
to accomplish the bold feat.

398
00:23:15,420 --> 00:23:17,920
The high scalers had to hang off the side of the canyon wall,

399
00:23:18,020 --> 00:23:21,060
sitting in bosun's chairs, suspended by ropes.

400
00:23:21,160 --> 00:23:23,360
They carried a 44-pound pneumatic drill.

401
00:23:23,460 --> 00:23:25,440
They drilled holes into the canyon wall,

402
00:23:25,540 --> 00:23:27,900
placed dynamite charges into those holes.

403
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:30,200
They then swung away as the dynamite exploded,

404
00:23:30,300 --> 00:23:32,140
swung back to the original positions,

405
00:23:32,240 --> 00:23:33,840
prying off the broken pieces of rock

406
00:23:33,841 --> 00:23:38,680
with steel levers. When dam construction began,

407
00:23:38,681 --> 00:23:42,720
government policy dictated that the high scalers and all employees

408
00:23:42,721 --> 00:23:46,720
should be out of the squatter style camps and into more acceptable living conditions.

409
00:23:49,360 --> 00:23:54,000
Six companies labored to provide housing for its workers as quickly as possible,

410
00:23:54,001 --> 00:23:57,440
and built Boulder City, seven miles from the work site.

411
00:23:59,040 --> 00:24:03,840
They built everything that a city should have. And the planning for Boulder City

412
00:24:03,841 --> 00:24:06,740
it was decided that it would be a full community.

413
00:24:06,840 --> 00:24:08,480
It would have a movie theater.

414
00:24:08,580 --> 00:24:09,980
It would have entertainment.

415
00:24:10,080 --> 00:24:12,080
It would have schools for the children.

416
00:24:12,180 --> 00:24:15,180
It would have churches for people to go to.

417
00:24:15,280 --> 00:24:18,180
Boulder City was normal in all senses,

418
00:24:18,280 --> 00:24:21,000
except that it was a government reservation.

419
00:24:21,100 --> 00:24:23,600
The Secretary of the Interior, Ray Wilbur,

420
00:24:23,700 --> 00:24:29,100
appointed Sims Ely to manage the city of 7,000 residents.

421
00:24:29,200 --> 00:24:33,840
A man who commanded respect, Sims Ely acted as general

422
00:24:33,841 --> 00:24:38,640
Mayor and Chamber of Commerce. He decided who would be given

423
00:24:38,641 --> 00:24:43,040
business licenses and what types of businesses they could have.

424
00:24:43,041 --> 00:24:46,080
Unlike many others during the Depression, people in Boulder

425
00:24:46,081 --> 00:24:50,080
City had some money to spend. But by choosing which businesses

426
00:24:50,081 --> 00:24:54,080
were allowed in town, Simsie Lee determined for his workers how

427
00:24:54,081 --> 00:24:57,080
to spend their money more responsibly.

428
00:24:57,081 --> 00:25:00,080
Six companies paid their men partially in script.

429
00:25:00,081 --> 00:25:03,080
This was money that was printed by six companies, and they could

430
00:25:03,081 --> 00:25:06,280
and they could only spend it at the company's store

431
00:25:06,380 --> 00:25:08,280
in Boulder City.

432
00:25:08,380 --> 00:25:11,280
Boulder City was a company-controlled town.

433
00:25:11,380 --> 00:25:14,620
As city manager, Sim Zeeley was also responsible for enforcing

434
00:25:14,720 --> 00:25:19,440
the law and had federal marshals on site to help him do it.

435
00:25:19,540 --> 00:25:23,440
Alcohol and gambling were not allowed within the city limits.

436
00:25:23,540 --> 00:25:27,540
But in nearby Las Vegas, just about everything was.

437
00:25:27,541 --> 00:25:29,540
to the price of the price.

438
00:25:32,540 --> 00:25:37,540
Las Vegas offered an escape from the rigid rules of Boulder City.

439
00:25:37,541 --> 00:25:40,540
Boulder fueled the commerce and growth of Las Vegas.

440
00:25:40,541 --> 00:25:43,540
And like the international attraction it is today,

441
00:25:43,541 --> 00:25:47,540
Las Vegas accepted all varieties of currency.

442
00:25:47,541 --> 00:25:50,540
The workers would go down to Las Vegas onto Block 16,

443
00:25:50,541 --> 00:25:52,540
which is the red light district, and they would drink,

444
00:25:52,541 --> 00:25:55,540
and they would gamble, and they would get serviced

445
00:25:55,541 --> 00:25:59,240
along Block 16, and they would pay for it with the script.

446
00:25:59,340 --> 00:26:01,940
Then the prostitutes take the script they earned

447
00:26:02,040 --> 00:26:06,280
and come back up to Boulder City with it and go shopping.

448
00:26:06,380 --> 00:26:08,980
Boulder City's location, only 30 miles

449
00:26:09,080 --> 00:26:11,780
from rowdy Las Vegas, emphasized the fact

450
00:26:11,880 --> 00:26:13,800
that it was a haven.

451
00:26:13,900 --> 00:26:16,200
There was very little crime in Boulder City,

452
00:26:16,300 --> 00:26:19,900
and to many it was a paradise, a sanctuary, safer

453
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:22,700
from the evils of the outside world.

454
00:26:22,800 --> 00:26:24,200
It was a mild town.

455
00:26:24,201 --> 00:26:26,000
It was perfect.

456
00:26:26,100 --> 00:26:27,840
It was meant to be perfect.

457
00:26:27,940 --> 00:26:31,340
You couldn't have any kind of alcohol.

458
00:26:31,440 --> 00:26:33,740
You better not.

459
00:26:33,840 --> 00:26:38,340
Boulder City offered security, shelter, and a full belly.

460
00:26:38,440 --> 00:26:41,560
The food was good, and there was plenty of it.

461
00:26:41,660 --> 00:26:44,560
When others were starving, workers in Boulder City

462
00:26:44,660 --> 00:26:48,460
feasted on three square meals a day.

463
00:26:48,560 --> 00:26:51,960
The Anderson Brothers mess hall served as many as 1,300 men

464
00:26:52,060 --> 00:26:53,960
per seating.

465
00:26:53,961 --> 00:26:58,260
and prepared as many as 6,000 meals a day.

466
00:26:58,360 --> 00:27:01,000
Boulder City was more than a construction camp.

467
00:27:01,100 --> 00:27:04,440
It was a place where people could get a new start.

468
00:27:04,540 --> 00:27:08,180
But Boulder City wasn't for everyone.

469
00:27:08,280 --> 00:27:10,240
It was supposed to be a perfect town,

470
00:27:10,340 --> 00:27:13,140
but the segregation was tremendous too.

471
00:27:13,240 --> 00:27:16,520
They had one black crew that worked on the dam.

472
00:27:16,620 --> 00:27:18,660
There was about 14 or 15 members.

473
00:27:18,760 --> 00:27:21,720
They could not even live in Boulder City.

474
00:27:23,960 --> 00:27:27,360
Fewer than 1% of DAM employees were African-American,

475
00:27:27,361 --> 00:27:31,860
and they were signed the least desirable positions.

476
00:27:31,861 --> 00:27:35,200
The new Secretary of the Interior, Harold Dickies,

477
00:27:35,201 --> 00:27:37,840
hoped to alter the situation.

478
00:27:37,841 --> 00:27:40,800
But he didn't have authority to change six companies' hiring

479
00:27:40,801 --> 00:27:42,220
practices.

480
00:27:42,221 --> 00:27:46,480
He did, however, have authority in Boulder City.

481
00:27:46,481 --> 00:27:50,980
He announced in 1933 that the few African-American employees

482
00:27:50,981 --> 00:27:52,460
could live there.

483
00:27:52,461 --> 00:27:55,260
it was a minor victory for those who found discrimination

484
00:27:55,261 --> 00:27:56,160
a way of life.

485
00:28:01,260 --> 00:28:04,060
For all the men, Hoover Dam offered work

486
00:28:04,061 --> 00:28:07,260
during the crushing Great Depression.

487
00:28:07,261 --> 00:28:11,400
Once the dam began to rise from the bottom of Black Canyon,

488
00:28:11,401 --> 00:28:13,560
those men would see they were part of something

489
00:28:13,561 --> 00:28:17,400
growing to historic proportions, something

490
00:28:17,401 --> 00:28:20,260
that would demand the development of new technologies

491
00:28:20,261 --> 00:28:24,760
before it could be completed.

492
00:28:24,761 --> 00:28:27,460
Herbert Hoover, champion and namesake of the dam,

493
00:28:27,461 --> 00:28:32,200
only visited it once during construction in 1932.

494
00:28:46,160 --> 00:28:48,560
More than once over the years, Hoover Dam

495
00:28:48,561 --> 00:28:52,560
something of an identity crisis.

496
00:28:52,660 --> 00:28:55,400
It was originally referred to as Boulder Dam,

497
00:28:55,500 --> 00:28:57,300
even though it ended up being built in black

498
00:28:57,400 --> 00:29:00,000
instead of Boulder Canyon.

499
00:29:00,100 --> 00:29:03,500
Construction began during Herbert Hoover's administration,

500
00:29:03,600 --> 00:29:06,520
and it became Hoover Dam.

501
00:29:06,620 --> 00:29:09,120
Herbert Hoover was very instrumental in getting

502
00:29:09,220 --> 00:29:12,120
this Black Canyon Project Act put through Congress

503
00:29:12,220 --> 00:29:13,920
so the dam could get built.

504
00:29:14,020 --> 00:29:16,320
But what happened was when the Roosevelt administration

505
00:29:16,321 --> 00:29:20,060
came into play, they didn't really care for Hubert Hoover,

506
00:29:20,160 --> 00:29:22,460
and they didn't like calling it Hoover Dam.

507
00:29:22,560 --> 00:29:26,560
So they made a statement calling it Boulder Dam.

508
00:29:26,660 --> 00:29:29,460
Even to this day, I'll have people come up to me and ask,

509
00:29:29,560 --> 00:29:31,360
where's Boulder Dam?

510
00:29:31,460 --> 00:29:34,780
It wouldn't be until 1947 that an act of Congress

511
00:29:34,880 --> 00:29:38,680
made Hoover Dam the official name.

512
00:29:38,780 --> 00:29:41,680
But fortunately, the great name debate didn't impede progress

513
00:29:41,780 --> 00:29:43,480
on the dam.

514
00:29:43,481 --> 00:29:47,720
On November 14, 1932, water began flowing through the great

515
00:29:47,820 --> 00:29:50,320
diversion tunnels.

516
00:29:50,420 --> 00:29:53,020
Men successfully forced the entire Colorado River

517
00:29:53,120 --> 00:29:56,520
from its ancient pen.

518
00:29:56,620 --> 00:30:00,240
Then the two temporary dams, called coffer dams,

519
00:30:00,340 --> 00:30:03,040
were erected to provide the dry work area.

520
00:30:03,140 --> 00:30:05,040
The coffer dams are both earthen.

521
00:30:05,140 --> 00:30:07,540
The one that is upriver is actually a larger one.

522
00:30:07,640 --> 00:30:09,440
It was one of the larger dams in the world

523
00:30:09,540 --> 00:30:12,480
when it was constructed almost 100 feet high,

524
00:30:12,481 --> 00:30:14,020
but 700 feet thick.

525
00:30:14,120 --> 00:30:16,520
It's actually thicker than the Hoover Dam is.

526
00:30:16,620 --> 00:30:18,820
Lower Cofford Dam needed to be, of course, much smaller

527
00:30:18,920 --> 00:30:20,820
because all it was doing was allowing water not

528
00:30:20,920 --> 00:30:22,420
to back up on the workers.

529
00:30:22,520 --> 00:30:24,920
Water was then pumped from between the dams

530
00:30:25,020 --> 00:30:27,620
and excavation began.

531
00:30:27,720 --> 00:30:29,740
Excavation was a monumental undertaking.

532
00:30:32,640 --> 00:30:34,940
More than 40 feet of rock, sand, and silt

533
00:30:35,040 --> 00:30:39,280
had to be removed from the now dry riverbank.

534
00:30:39,281 --> 00:30:42,680
Steam shovels dredged a half million cubic yards of material

535
00:30:42,780 --> 00:30:45,920
in preparation for pouring concrete.

536
00:30:46,020 --> 00:30:50,460
And on June 6, 1933, the first bucket of concrete poured

537
00:30:50,560 --> 00:30:52,560
into the form at the bottom of the canyon.

538
00:30:56,040 --> 00:30:57,860
Producing enough concrete for the dam

539
00:30:57,960 --> 00:30:59,900
was a problem in itself.

540
00:31:00,000 --> 00:31:03,940
Hoover Dam required nearly as much concrete as all previous

541
00:31:04,040 --> 00:31:05,840
Bureau of Reclamation dams combined.

542
00:31:09,280 --> 00:31:12,120
A nearby riverbed provided the gravel.

543
00:31:12,220 --> 00:31:14,680
Men loaded material into train cars

544
00:31:14,780 --> 00:31:16,980
and transported it to the separating plant,

545
00:31:17,080 --> 00:31:20,320
where it was divided by size.

546
00:31:20,420 --> 00:31:23,020
Trains then hauled gravel to the two mixing facilities

547
00:31:23,120 --> 00:31:25,320
built to produce the tremendous amount of concrete.

548
00:31:28,740 --> 00:31:31,800
Night and day, concrete left the mixing plant,

549
00:31:31,900 --> 00:31:35,400
truckload after truckload, bucket after bucket,

550
00:31:35,500 --> 00:31:37,040
for placement in the dam forms.

551
00:31:39,280 --> 00:31:41,920
It's not one monolithic piece.

552
00:31:42,020 --> 00:31:43,920
It's poured more like a kid's LEGO toy

553
00:31:44,020 --> 00:31:45,920
is locked together like that.

554
00:31:46,020 --> 00:31:48,220
And they poured grout in between those.

555
00:31:48,320 --> 00:31:50,420
So they poured it in individual blocks.

556
00:31:54,520 --> 00:31:58,540
The dam was built up in forms from 25 to 60 feet square

557
00:31:58,640 --> 00:32:01,540
and 5 feet deep.

558
00:32:01,640 --> 00:32:04,380
If they had poured it as one massive block,

559
00:32:04,480 --> 00:32:06,980
not only would it be inclined to crack,

560
00:32:06,981 --> 00:32:12,380
But it would take over 125 years to completely harden.

561
00:32:12,480 --> 00:32:14,620
The chemical reaction of hardening concrete

562
00:32:14,720 --> 00:32:16,620
also produces heat.

563
00:32:16,720 --> 00:32:20,220
Too much heat would cause the huge concrete blocks to crack,

564
00:32:20,320 --> 00:32:23,220
so steel pipes were inserted into the wet concrete,

565
00:32:23,320 --> 00:32:26,240
and cold water pumped through them to reduce cracking.

566
00:32:28,300 --> 00:32:32,000
A project of this magnitude and such strict deadlines

567
00:32:32,100 --> 00:32:35,680
required many innovative construction techniques.

568
00:32:35,681 --> 00:32:38,580
Frank Crow pioneered the extensive use of cableways

569
00:32:38,680 --> 00:32:42,180
on earlier projects, but never before had cableways

570
00:32:42,280 --> 00:32:45,820
been used so efficiently.

571
00:32:45,920 --> 00:32:49,320
From 1 8th of a mile above, cableway operators

572
00:32:49,420 --> 00:32:53,540
choreographed a ballet of man and machine.

573
00:32:53,640 --> 00:32:58,040
Train cars transported concrete buckets from the mixing plant.

574
00:32:58,140 --> 00:33:01,380
Cableway operators lifted the 22-ton buckets and sent them

575
00:33:01,480 --> 00:33:05,680
gliding to the appropriate form.

576
00:33:05,681 --> 00:33:09,180
There, men released the safety locks,

577
00:33:09,181 --> 00:33:13,920
and 16 tons of concrete poured into the form.

578
00:33:13,921 --> 00:33:18,220
Activity progressed 24 hours a day, seven days a week,

579
00:33:18,221 --> 00:33:22,920
as workers poured up to 1,100 buckets of concrete per day.

580
00:33:22,921 --> 00:33:26,340
This kind of dangerous work led to another innovation

581
00:33:26,341 --> 00:33:27,940
at the dam site.

582
00:33:27,941 --> 00:33:31,180
We believe that this is where the hard hat was invented,

583
00:33:31,181 --> 00:33:34,180
that everybody was able to get the dams.

584
00:33:34,181 --> 00:33:37,640
was invented that everybody uses nowadays.

585
00:33:37,641 --> 00:33:40,380
The construction workers had been faced with debris

586
00:33:40,381 --> 00:33:44,080
falling on them from above, and they realized

587
00:33:44,081 --> 00:33:46,540
that they were going to have to do something.

588
00:33:46,541 --> 00:33:51,240
And so what they did was they put two baseball caps together

589
00:33:51,241 --> 00:33:55,420
like this with the bill going this way and this way.

590
00:33:55,421 --> 00:33:59,080
And then they dipped it in tar and let it harden

591
00:33:59,081 --> 00:34:01,480
and dipped it in tar and let it harden.

592
00:34:01,481 --> 00:34:03,140
And these were all homemade hats.

593
00:34:03,141 --> 00:34:06,280
This company didn't have anything to do with this.

594
00:34:06,281 --> 00:34:10,440
But when several men wearing these so-called hard-boil hats

595
00:34:10,441 --> 00:34:13,620
were saved from falling debris, six companies purchased

596
00:34:13,621 --> 00:34:17,020
thousands of them for their employees.

597
00:34:17,021 --> 00:34:20,220
As the dam grew taller, so did the tail surrounding

598
00:34:20,221 --> 00:34:21,660
the project.

599
00:34:21,661 --> 00:34:25,420
Despite rumors, no one is buried in the concrete.

600
00:34:25,421 --> 00:34:28,340
A decomposing body would have threatened the integrity

601
00:34:28,341 --> 00:34:29,340
of the dam.

602
00:34:29,341 --> 00:34:32,700
Besides, an average pour only increased the concrete's depth

603
00:34:32,701 --> 00:34:37,200
inches. While the concrete was being poured,

604
00:34:37,300 --> 00:34:39,240
there were several ancillary projects happening

605
00:34:39,340 --> 00:34:44,040
simultaneously. The four steel-reinforced

606
00:34:44,140 --> 00:34:48,740
concrete water intake towers were built behind the dam.

607
00:34:48,840 --> 00:34:52,160
Mounted nearly halfway up the sides of the canyon, the towers

608
00:34:52,260 --> 00:34:56,260
only had to be 395 feet tall.

609
00:34:56,360 --> 00:35:00,060
Manufacturing the giant steel penstocks needed to feed water

610
00:35:00,061 --> 00:35:02,860
from the intake towers to the turbines,

611
00:35:02,960 --> 00:35:06,860
tested the resources of the day.

612
00:35:06,960 --> 00:35:10,200
The 30-foot diameter pipes were too large to ship,

613
00:35:10,300 --> 00:35:12,700
so an entire fabrication facility was erected

614
00:35:12,800 --> 00:35:15,800
close to the dam.

615
00:35:15,900 --> 00:35:19,520
Giant presses rolled 2 and 3-quarter inch steel plates

616
00:35:19,620 --> 00:35:25,020
into 11-foot pipe sections, weighing 80 tons each.

617
00:35:25,120 --> 00:35:30,060
Two pieces combined to make one colossal section 22 feet long.

618
00:35:30,061 --> 00:35:34,060
Tractors moved the enormous sections to the canyon rim,

619
00:35:34,160 --> 00:35:36,700
where the largest cableway floated them

620
00:35:36,800 --> 00:35:39,000
to the base of the dam.

621
00:35:39,100 --> 00:35:41,300
Each section was joined to other sections,

622
00:35:41,400 --> 00:35:43,900
forming over one mile of welded pipe.

623
00:35:46,000 --> 00:35:49,120
On each side of the dam, engineers designed a spillway

624
00:35:49,220 --> 00:35:51,820
to protect it in times of great flooding.

625
00:35:51,920 --> 00:35:53,320
If the water ever got high enough,

626
00:35:53,420 --> 00:35:56,120
or it would endanger the dam by going over the top of the dam,

627
00:35:56,220 --> 00:35:58,120
which would destroy the power plant below,

628
00:35:58,121 --> 00:36:00,660
water actually goes over the sides,

629
00:36:00,760 --> 00:36:03,760
and it goes down through large 50-foot diameter tubes

630
00:36:03,860 --> 00:36:06,060
that push the water around the side of the project.

631
00:36:06,160 --> 00:36:09,560
And those have been used twice in history, once in 1941

632
00:36:09,660 --> 00:36:12,060
and again in 1983.

633
00:36:12,160 --> 00:36:14,780
That year, water flowed over the spillways

634
00:36:14,880 --> 00:36:18,680
at 52,000 cubic feet per second.

635
00:36:18,780 --> 00:36:22,520
Despite the record water flow, the spillways performed just

636
00:36:22,620 --> 00:36:26,280
as their designers had intended and helped minimize flooding

637
00:36:26,281 --> 00:36:32,280
flooding downriver. When the spillways were constructed,

638
00:36:32,281 --> 00:36:35,620
the dam was one step closer to completion.

639
00:36:35,621 --> 00:36:39,020
But there was a lot at risk, and only one way to test the

640
00:36:39,021 --> 00:36:42,520
structure, fill Lake Mead and see if the dam would be able to

641
00:36:42,521 --> 00:36:46,040
hold back the pressure exerted by trillions of gallons of

642
00:36:46,041 --> 00:36:51,980
water. From 1939 to 1949, Hoover Dam's

643
00:36:51,981 --> 00:36:54,840
power plant was the world's largest hydroelectric

644
00:36:54,841 --> 00:36:59,240
installation, it is still one of America's biggest facilities.

645
00:37:11,900 --> 00:37:14,900
When Hoover Dam was finished in 1935,

646
00:37:14,901 --> 00:37:18,800
it was the biggest dam in the world, which under Frank Crowe's

647
00:37:18,801 --> 00:37:22,200
leadership was completed faster and for less money than initially

648
00:37:22,201 --> 00:37:23,900
planned.

649
00:37:23,901 --> 00:37:27,600
to accomplish what Frank Crow did two years ahead of schedule

650
00:37:27,700 --> 00:37:29,500
and under budget.

651
00:37:29,600 --> 00:37:31,640
Had to be a tremendous individual,

652
00:37:31,740 --> 00:37:34,440
maybe not loved by everybody, but certainly

653
00:37:34,540 --> 00:37:38,340
he set out to build the dam, and he accomplished that.

654
00:37:38,440 --> 00:37:42,060
Over 21,000 workers had labored on the massive project.

655
00:37:42,160 --> 00:37:44,860
For them, the end product became more than just a combination

656
00:37:44,960 --> 00:37:47,160
of sweat and concrete.

657
00:37:47,260 --> 00:37:49,860
At the time we completed it, I just

658
00:37:49,960 --> 00:37:52,400
felt that I had been a part of something

659
00:37:52,401 --> 00:37:54,240
that was just tremendous.

660
00:37:54,340 --> 00:37:58,540
On September 30, 1935, President Franklin Delano

661
00:37:58,640 --> 00:38:01,840
Roosevelt came to dedicate the dam that so many had worked so

662
00:38:01,940 --> 00:38:04,740
hard to complete.

663
00:38:04,840 --> 00:38:07,040
Four months before Roosevelt's dedication,

664
00:38:07,140 --> 00:38:10,260
Lake Mead began filling.

665
00:38:10,360 --> 00:38:13,660
It took six years to reach maximum capacity,

666
00:38:13,760 --> 00:38:15,960
and the mighty structure successfully held back

667
00:38:16,060 --> 00:38:17,560
trillions of gallons of water.

668
00:38:17,561 --> 00:38:22,560
There was no shortage of pride in what was accomplished

669
00:38:22,660 --> 00:38:24,300
in Black Canyon.

670
00:38:24,400 --> 00:38:29,100
And in 1938, sculptor Oscar J.W. Hanson

671
00:38:29,200 --> 00:38:31,600
completed a memorial next to the dam,

672
00:38:31,700 --> 00:38:34,520
featuring two statues called the Winged Figures

673
00:38:34,620 --> 00:38:36,120
of the Republic.

674
00:38:36,220 --> 00:38:39,020
He wanted to make a statement about the workers

675
00:38:39,120 --> 00:38:40,220
on the project.

676
00:38:40,320 --> 00:38:42,660
And that statement was built by man, the Hoover Dam,

677
00:38:42,760 --> 00:38:44,420
but not the average man.

678
00:38:44,520 --> 00:38:45,960
They're 30 feet tall.

679
00:38:45,961 --> 00:38:48,860
They weigh 4 1⁄2 tons of statuary bronze.

680
00:38:48,960 --> 00:38:50,960
And you notice they've aged to a lovely green color,

681
00:38:51,060 --> 00:38:52,460
except for the toes.

682
00:38:52,560 --> 00:38:54,460
It is considered good luck in Hoover Dam folklore

683
00:38:54,560 --> 00:38:57,300
to rub the toes.

684
00:38:57,400 --> 00:38:59,300
Inlaid on the floor around the statues

685
00:38:59,400 --> 00:39:02,720
are 209 disks of various sizes.

686
00:39:02,820 --> 00:39:05,220
They indicate the positions of the brightest stars

687
00:39:05,320 --> 00:39:08,720
and planets that were visible from this exact location

688
00:39:08,820 --> 00:39:13,720
at 8.56 PM on the 30th of September, 1935,

689
00:39:13,721 --> 00:39:16,520
the day of President Roosevelt's dedication.

690
00:39:16,620 --> 00:39:19,220
And the goal of the sculptor, Oscar J.W. Hansen,

691
00:39:19,320 --> 00:39:22,020
was to go ahead and provide something to what he called

692
00:39:22,120 --> 00:39:23,460
future visitors.

693
00:39:23,560 --> 00:39:25,660
And his thinking was that we, as human beings,

694
00:39:25,760 --> 00:39:27,060
may not be here.

695
00:39:27,160 --> 00:39:29,500
He assumed that those future visitors would be able to read

696
00:39:29,600 --> 00:39:32,140
a star chart, and they could tell the position of the stars

697
00:39:32,240 --> 00:39:35,380
when they come to visit versus where they were in 1935,

698
00:39:35,480 --> 00:39:37,580
and be able to estimate how long ago the construction

699
00:39:37,680 --> 00:39:39,820
was finished.

700
00:39:39,920 --> 00:39:42,080
When construction ended, most men

701
00:39:42,081 --> 00:39:44,540
Most men left to work on other dams,

702
00:39:44,640 --> 00:39:46,880
but many stayed to work on the powerhouse

703
00:39:46,980 --> 00:39:49,180
and finishing touches.

704
00:39:49,280 --> 00:39:51,420
Unlike other construction camps,

705
00:39:51,520 --> 00:39:53,520
Boulder City didn't dry up.

706
00:39:53,620 --> 00:39:59,000
It remained a reservation until 1959, when it incorporated.

707
00:39:59,100 --> 00:40:04,040
While alcohol was finally offered for sale in 1969,

708
00:40:04,140 --> 00:40:07,480
Boulder City remains the only city in Nevada that doesn't

709
00:40:07,580 --> 00:40:08,340
allow gambling.

710
00:40:12,080 --> 00:40:15,680
Hoover Dam quickly became an indispensable component

711
00:40:15,780 --> 00:40:19,160
of the successful development of America's southwest

712
00:40:19,260 --> 00:40:22,860
that had to be protected.

713
00:40:22,960 --> 00:40:24,900
On a hill above the dam are the remains

714
00:40:25,000 --> 00:40:29,300
of one of the gun emplacements built during World War II.

715
00:40:29,400 --> 00:40:31,940
As a supplier of power to defense plants,

716
00:40:32,040 --> 00:40:35,800
the dam was considered a primary military target.

717
00:40:35,900 --> 00:40:40,400
Since September 11, 2001, new and not to be disclosed

718
00:40:40,401 --> 00:40:44,240
security measures have been instituted in and around the dam.

719
00:40:48,640 --> 00:40:50,960
The structure itself has been remarkably steadfast

720
00:40:50,961 --> 00:40:53,760
since it was built. Over the decades,

721
00:40:53,761 --> 00:40:56,400
inspectors have walked the interior tunnels

722
00:40:56,401 --> 00:40:59,200
and marked even the slightest crack in the concrete,

723
00:40:59,201 --> 00:41:00,800
but none has proved significant.

724
00:41:04,640 --> 00:41:07,640
Large doors similar to those inside submarines

725
00:41:07,641 --> 00:41:09,600
were put in place to protect the power plant

726
00:41:09,601 --> 00:41:14,100
from a possible flood should one of the huge water pipes rupture.

727
00:41:14,101 --> 00:41:17,100
They have never been needed.

728
00:41:17,101 --> 00:41:19,100
One of the upgrades in safety technology

729
00:41:19,101 --> 00:41:21,840
is to add some additional earthquake sensors.

730
00:41:21,841 --> 00:41:24,100
We've experienced over 20,000 earthquakes here

731
00:41:24,101 --> 00:41:27,100
at the dam and the power plants, the highest being a 5.6

732
00:41:27,101 --> 00:41:30,600
on the Richter scale, with no damage to the dam, power plant,

733
00:41:30,601 --> 00:41:32,400
or any piping so far.

734
00:41:32,401 --> 00:41:35,600
People estimate that the dam is able to withstand an 8.6

735
00:41:35,601 --> 00:41:38,100
on the Richter scale.

736
00:41:38,101 --> 00:41:39,860
While the concrete structure of the dam

737
00:41:39,861 --> 00:41:42,100
has remained virtually unchanged,

738
00:41:42,101 --> 00:41:43,800
the moving parts in the power plant

739
00:41:43,801 --> 00:41:46,980
have required maintenance and upgrades.

740
00:41:46,981 --> 00:41:49,640
Original turbine runners we had here were cast iron.

741
00:41:49,641 --> 00:41:52,820
They've got a life of approximately 40 to 50 years.

742
00:41:52,821 --> 00:41:55,860
So beginning in the mid-'80s, 50 year into the life

743
00:41:55,861 --> 00:41:57,620
of the project, they were replaced

744
00:41:57,621 --> 00:41:59,120
with the stainless steel runners.

745
00:41:59,121 --> 00:42:02,120
Up above the generator, where all the electricity was produced,

746
00:42:02,121 --> 00:42:04,500
back at the same time in the mid-'80s,

747
00:42:04,501 --> 00:42:06,400
we took the old generator, which again,

748
00:42:06,401 --> 00:42:09,900
Then after 30, 40 years, insulation wears out,

749
00:42:10,000 --> 00:42:11,240
breaks down.

750
00:42:11,340 --> 00:42:13,200
We did a complete overhaul of generators

751
00:42:13,300 --> 00:42:16,780
to then produce new outputs from about 87 megawatts

752
00:42:16,880 --> 00:42:17,780
up to 130.

753
00:42:17,880 --> 00:42:19,820
So we got quite a bit more power out of the same

754
00:42:19,920 --> 00:42:21,020
by just upgrading the unit.

755
00:42:24,160 --> 00:42:27,860
Water continues to be the dam's most precious commodity.

756
00:42:27,960 --> 00:42:30,860
And the US is not the only beneficiary.

757
00:42:30,960 --> 00:42:36,400
Mexico receives 1.5 million acre feet of water a year.

758
00:42:36,401 --> 00:42:39,040
While Hoover Dam does supply much needed water,

759
00:42:39,140 --> 00:42:42,600
like all dams, it blocks the natural flow of a river

760
00:42:42,700 --> 00:42:45,740
and has an impact on plants and animals.

761
00:42:45,840 --> 00:42:48,140
More fish live here than ever could before.

762
00:42:48,240 --> 00:42:51,440
More animals, mountain sheep, birds, mountain lions,

763
00:42:51,540 --> 00:42:54,560
because the one scarce element, the one thing that was missing

764
00:42:54,660 --> 00:42:56,760
from this environment, water.

765
00:42:56,860 --> 00:42:58,560
But altering the natural habitat

766
00:42:58,660 --> 00:43:01,060
has also led to the absence of many species

767
00:43:01,160 --> 00:43:03,460
within the Colorado River.

768
00:43:03,560 --> 00:43:06,400
Some native fish have found reproduction more

769
00:43:06,401 --> 00:43:08,200
in the colder waters of Lake Mead.

770
00:43:11,600 --> 00:43:13,200
The engineers that designed Hoover Dam

771
00:43:13,300 --> 00:43:14,800
could not have foreseen all the ways

772
00:43:14,900 --> 00:43:16,700
the completed project would affect people

773
00:43:16,800 --> 00:43:17,540
and the environment.

774
00:43:21,340 --> 00:43:23,360
But judged by its original goals,

775
00:43:23,460 --> 00:43:26,260
Hoover Dam is a tremendous success story.

776
00:43:26,360 --> 00:43:28,960
From the close-knit community of Boulder City

777
00:43:29,060 --> 00:43:33,200
to bountiful harvests in once barren landscapes,

778
00:43:33,300 --> 00:43:36,160
Hoover Dam has changed the course of a river

779
00:43:36,161 --> 00:43:39,100
and the course of people's lives.

780
00:43:39,101 --> 00:43:43,400
Here we are, almost 70 years after the dam was completed.

781
00:43:43,401 --> 00:43:46,700
The dam has never missed one single day of providing water

782
00:43:46,701 --> 00:43:48,800
or electricity to its customers.

783
00:43:48,801 --> 00:43:52,400
And some say we'll still be here serving the public 2,000 years

784
00:43:52,401 --> 00:43:54,420
from today.

785
00:43:54,421 --> 00:43:58,420
I think it symbolizes what the American people were about

786
00:43:58,421 --> 00:44:01,220
in that particular time in our history.

787
00:44:01,221 --> 00:44:03,420
We were moving to new frontiers.

788
00:44:03,421 --> 00:44:04,920
We were settling the West.

789
00:44:04,921 --> 00:44:07,120
We were building a nation.

790
00:44:07,220 --> 00:44:08,860
We were building a life.

791
00:44:08,960 --> 00:44:13,760
And the attitude was, we can do this.

792
00:44:13,860 --> 00:44:16,660
The men who built Hoover Dam had not only

793
00:44:16,760 --> 00:44:18,760
accomplished what they set out to do,

794
00:44:18,860 --> 00:44:21,900
but created what has been cited as one of the seven

795
00:44:22,000 --> 00:44:23,900
engineering wonders of the world.

